{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"agent_message","text":"I submitted the mitigation, but I couldn't reproduce the degradation, so the fix is a best guess rather than a confirmed root cause.\n\n- **What I checked:** all application pods are Running with no restarts. Frontend requests for hotels, recommendations, login and reservation return 200 in about 5–10 ms. A 200-request concurrent burst peaked at 82 ms. Jaeger shows no traces over 300 ms in the last 30 minutes. Rate and search metrics show zero rejections, expirations, timeouts or failures. MongoDB `admin` privileges on `rate-db` and `geo-db` are intact.\n- **Only anomaly:** the `audit-log-archiver` CronJob, created after the app deployed, launches a pod every minute. Its fluent-bit sidecar never exits, so pods pile up at 1/2 Ready and the jobs never complete.\n- **Action taken:** I suspended the CronJob with `kubectl patch` (`spec.suspend: true`). I did not delete it or its existing pods.\n- **Not verified:** I found no evidence that the CronJob caused user-facing latency, and I couldn't observe the degradation before or after the change.\n\nTo undo the suspension, run `kubectl patch cronjob audit-log-archiver -n hotel-reservation -p '{\"spec\":{\"suspend\":false}}'`. The stuck pods can be cleaned up separately."}}
